#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""bromure-sandboxd — the root launcher that owns the sandboxed tmux server.

This is Bromure's answer to OpenShell's supervisor/workload split, and to the
design question in §1 of the contract: *who applies the sandbox, and to what?*

The shape of the problem. `bromure-agentd.py` runs as `ubuntu` under systemd and
starts one tmux server; every agent and every user shell lives in a pane of that
server. agentd cannot setuid, so it cannot apply `run_as_user` to anything, and
it cannot open Landlock path fds for a mode-700 directory. Worse, a sandbox
applied by agentd to itself would cover agentd — including the vsock channels
and the host bridges, which must stay outside.

So the boundary is the tmux server, and something with root has to put it there:

    agentd (ubuntu, unsandboxed)
      └── sudo bromure-sandboxd            <- root, this program
            ├── reads /mnt/bromure-meta/openshell-sandbox.json
            ├── opens Landlock path fds  AS ROOT  (OpenShell phase 1)
            ├── setsid()                          (own session; see below)
            ├── drop_privileges()                 (OpenShell's order, verbatim)
            ├── no_new_privs + restrict_self + child filter + main filter
            └── exec tmux -S … new-session -d     (server inherits all of it)

Everything the agent can ever reach is a descendant of that tmux server, so
everything the agent can ever reach is inside the ruleset. agentd stays outside
it, which is what lets it keep talking to the host.

Why the tmux server and not each pane: a per-pane sandbox would be applied by
the tmux server, which would then be *outside* it — and the tmux server is
reachable from every pane through its own control socket. The server is the
narrowest boundary that has nothing trusted inside it.

Why `setsid()`: the sandboxed tree shares a uid with agentd by default (see
DESIGN.md on virtiofs), so OpenShell's child self-protection filter is what
stops the workload attacking its supervisor. That filter's blanket denial of
`kill(0, …)` and `kill(<negative>, …)` would break job control in every
interactive pane, so it is narrowed to the supervisor's TGID and PGID and
`kill(-1)`. `setsid()` is what makes the narrowing sound: the sandbox gets its
own session, so the only process groups it can name are its own.

Exit codes: 0 applied (or deliberately skipped), 1 refused because a
hard_requirement could not be met, 2 misconfiguration. The status written to
STATUS_PATH is authoritative either way — attestd reads it and tells the host.
"""

